.TH yacc "" "Command"
.SH NAME
yacc \- parser generator
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fByacc [\fIoptions\fB] \fIfile\fR
.br
\fBcc y.tab.c [\-ly]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
yacc -- parser generator
Usage:	yacc [ options ] file
	cc y.tab.c [ -ly ]
Options:
	-d	Enable debugging output (implies '-v')
	-hdr	Next argument is name of header file (default: 'y.tab.h')
	-l	Next argument is name of listfile (default: 'y.output')
	-st	Print statistics on standard output
	-v	Verbose (extra output in listfile)
After each of the following, the next argument is a number to reset table size:
	-nterms	Nonterminal symbols (default: 100)
	-prods	Productions or rules (default: 175)
	-states	States (default: 300)
	-terms	Terminal symbols (default: 300)
	-types	Types (default: 10)
.HE
.B yacc
reads a grammar description from
.I file
(or, if
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
from the standard input) and writes an LALR(1) parser for it, in C, to
.BR y.tab.c ,
together with a header of token definitions in
.BR y.tab.h .
.B y.tab.c
is produced by copying the parser skeleton
.B /lib/yyparse.c
and splicing in the generated tables and semantic actions; the result
is compiled and linked like any other C source, typically with the
.B \-ly
library, which supplies run-time support (such as a default
.BR main ())
that
.B yacc
does not generate directly.
Any non-option argument is taken as
.IR file ;
options and the grammar file may appear in any order.
.PP
Table sizes for the number of terminal symbols, non-terminal symbols,
.B %type
tags, productions, and parser states are fixed when
.B yacc
starts; it reports a fatal error and stops if the grammar exceeds the
current limit for any of them.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Produce extra debugging output in the listing file; implies
.BR \-v .
.IP "\fB\-hdr\fR \fIfile\fR"
Write the token-definition header to
.I file
instead of the default
.BR y.tab.h .
.IP "\fB\-l\fR \fIfile\fR"
Write the verbose listing to
.I file
instead of the default
.BR y.output ;
implies
.BR \-v .
.IP \fB\-st\fR
Print grammar statistics on the standard output.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Produce a verbose listing file describing the states and conflicts of
the generated parser.
.IP "\fB\-prods\fR \fIn\fR"
Reset the maximum number of productions (rules) to
.IR n .
.IP "\fB\-terms\fR \fIn\fR"
Reset the maximum number of terminal symbols to
.IR n .
.IP "\fB\-nterms\fR \fIn\fR"
Reset the maximum number of non-terminal symbols to
.IR n .
.IP "\fB\-states\fR \fIn\fR"
Reset the maximum number of parser states to
.IR n .
.IP "\fB\-types\fR \fIn\fR"
Reset the maximum number of
.B %type
tags to
.IR n .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 18n
\fBy.tab.c\fR	generated parser source
\fBy.tab.h\fR	generated token definitions (default name)
\fBy.output\fR	verbose listing, with \-v or \-l (default name)
\fB/lib/yyparse.c\fR	parser skeleton copied into y.tab.c
\fB/usr/include/action.h\fR	action-table constants needed to compile y.tab.c
\fB/lib/liby.a\fR	run-time support library, linked with \-ly
\fB/tmp/ya*\fR, \fB/tmp/yo*\fR	temporary action and option files
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B lex
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B yacc
reports each grammar error with the input line number and a
description; a fatal error (for example a missing "%%" or an unreadable
grammar file) stops it immediately, and any error at all suppresses
generation of the parser tables.
.B yacc
also writes a count of shift/reduce and reduce/reduce conflicts.
.SH NOTES
.B yacc
in this tree always writes
.B y.tab.h
on every run, whether or not
.B \-d
was given.
.PP
The compiled-in default for the maximum number of terminal symbols is
150
.RB ( MAXTERM
in
.BR yacc.h ),
not 300 as given above (the other defaults shown -- 100
non-terminals, 175 productions, 300 states, 10 types -- agree with the
source).
Also, an explicit
.B \-terms
value of 160 or more makes
.B yacc
stop with the fatal error
.BR "get ciaran to change alloc for lsets" ,
since the lookahead bitset is sized for at most 159 terminal symbols.
